  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA much cooler airmass has settled into the Ohio Valley today and \nthis will remain in place across the region through the rest of the \nweek. \n\nThere remains a fair amount of uncertainty for the systems that will\naffect much of the eastern U.S later this week. There is fairly \nwidespread discrepancies on how the southern and northern stream \nsystems will phase with each other and just how much cold air will \neventually be pulled into our area with the northern system. \n\nAs the systems begin to phase, pcpn will likely spread up into our \narea late Wednesday night and into Thanksgiving day and then linger \nthrough Thursday night before tapering off on Friday. Pcpn should be\nall rain during the day on Friday, but the models do appear to be \ntrending somewhat cooler with the cold air aloft for Thursday night. \nThis could lead to a better chance of some snow mixing in during the \nnight time hours. The 06Z ECMWF ensemble mean is suggesting some \nlight accumulations across our area Thursday night and the 12Z \noperational GFS has trended to more snow. We will have to keep an \neye on model trends as we get closer, but for now, given the \nuncertainty and with ground temps being warm, will continue to \ndownplay it at this point.",
